{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The adapter calls `lm(messages=inputs, **lm_kwargs)`, so:\n- `lm_kwargs` (the config, which now includes `prediction` when valid) lands in the LM's `**kwargs`.\n- `inputs` get formatted into `messages` — the raw `inputs` dict isn't passed to the LM, so my assertion `\"prediction\" not in sync_lm.calls[0][\"kwargs\"].get(\"inputs\", {})` would trivially pass... Actually the LM receives `messages=...` and `**lm_kwargs`; there's no `inputs` key in kwargs. The `.get(\"inputs\", {})` assertion is meaningless — it would always pass. Better: assert the messages don't include the prediction content. Since the signature is \"question -> answer\" and prediction isn't a signature field, the adapter ignores it anyway when formatting... so that's also trivially true.\n\nThe most meaningful assertions:\n1. `kwargs[\"prediction\"] == prediction` — it reached the LM as a config parameter. ✓\n2. For invalid predictions: `\"prediction\" not in lm.calls[0][\"kwargs\"]` — it was NOT moved to config. ✓\n3. For valid predictions, the trace/inputs check: I can check `dspy.settings.trace` — after the call, the trace entry contains `{**kwargs}` inputs. If prediction was properly popped, the trace inputs shouldn't contain \"prediction\" key. Let me check how trace is captured: `_forward_postprocess` appends `(self, {**kwargs}, pred)` to `settings.trace` when trace is not None. I can use `dspy.settings.context(trace=...)`? Let me check how settings.trace works — `settings.trace` is a list when inside a context. Actually, let me check `settings.context`:\n\nIn `dspy/dsp/utils/settings.py`, `context()` — trace=... let me check. There's `dspy.utils.settings` with `trace` list. The trace is enabled in evaluate etc. Let me just check if `settings.trace` defaults to a list.\n\nActually simpler and more precise: directly test `_forward_preprocess` unit-wise for the removal from kwargs, plus end-to-end LM-level assertions. But `_forward_preprocess` is an internal method; testing it directly is fine for a unit test but the repo style seems to be end-to-end-ish. Let me check settings.trace usage quickly.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The adapter calls `lm(messages=inputs, **lm_kwargs)`, so:\\n- `lm_kwargs` (the config, which now includes `prediction` when valid) lands in the LM's `**kwargs`.\\n- `inputs` get formatted into `messages` — the raw `inputs` dict isn't passed to the LM, so my assertion `\\\"prediction\\\" not in sync_lm.calls[0][\\\"kwargs\\\"].get(\\\"inputs\\\", {})` would trivially pass... Actually the LM receives `messages=...` and `**lm_kwargs`; there's no `inputs` key in kwargs. The `.get(\\\"inputs\\\", {})` assertion is meaningless — it would always pass. Better: assert the messages don't include the prediction content. Since the signature is \\\"question -> answer\\\" and prediction isn't a signature field, the adapter ignores it anyway when formatting... so that's also trivially true.\\n\\nThe most meaningful assertions:\\n1. `kwargs[\\\"prediction\\\"] == prediction` — it reached the LM as a config parameter. ✓\\n2. For invalid predictions: `\\\"prediction\\\" not in lm.calls[0][\\\"kwargs\\\"]` — it was NOT moved to config. ✓\\n3. For valid predictions, the trace/inputs check: I can check `dspy.settings.trace` — after the call, the trace entry contains `{**kwargs}` inputs. If prediction was properly popped, the trace inputs shouldn't contain \\\"prediction\\\" key. Let me check how trace is captured: `_forward_postprocess` appends `(self, {**kwargs}, pred)` to `settings.trace` when trace is not None. I can use `dspy.settings.context(trace=...)`? Let me check how settings.trace works — `settings.trace` is a list when inside a context. Actually, let me check `settings.context`:\\n\\nIn `dspy/dsp/utils/settings.py`, `context()` — trace=... let me check. There's `dspy.utils.settings` with `trace` list. The trace is enabled in evaluate etc. Let me just check if `settings.trace` defaults to a list.\\n\\nActually simpler and more precise: directly test `_forward_preprocess` unit-wise for the removal from kwargs, plus end-to-end LM-level assertions. But `_forward_preprocess` is an internal method; testing it directly is fine for a unit test but the repo style seems to be end-to-end-ish. Let me check settings.trace usage quickly.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"The adapter passes `lm_kwargs` as `**kwargs` to the LM, so my assertions hold. But the `\"inputs\"` key assertion is meaningless — let me replace it with a trace-based check that verifies `prediction` was removed from the processed inputs:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01a06878b89a76a09cbde99d","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"trace\" /testbed/dspy/dsp/utils/settings.py | head; grep -rn \"settings.trace\\b\" /testbed/dspy/primitives/program.py 2>/dev/null | head -5"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"harbor-endpoint","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":19158,"output":602,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":494,"totalTokens":19760,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788459126392,"responseId":"gen-1788459126-SBd3Bpj79fCEZBLxL0IR","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
